hypotheses.statement:
At an interior point where $\,\mathrm dt=0$ and $\mathop{\mathrm{Hess}}t\ge0$, $$\label{n3:deformation:floor-gauss}
 \tfrac12e^{4t}\mathop{\mathrm{Scal}}_{\hat g}
 \le \tfrac12\mathop{\mathrm{Scal}}_g-v\mathop{\mathrm{Ric}}_g(e,e)+\mathcal S(H^f),$$ where, for a symmetric tensor $A$, $$\label{n3:deformation:gauss-quadratic}
 \begin{split}
 \mathcal S(A)={}&\tfrac14(\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}_\perp A)^2
 -\tfrac12|A_{\perp\perp}^{\operatorname{tf}}|^2\\
 &+dA_{ee}\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}_\perp A-d|A_{e\perp}|^2.
 \end{split}$$ There are universal $c_*,\eta_*>0$ and polynomial constants $C_N$ such that, at $\,\mathrm dt=0$, $$\label{n3:deformation:floor-algebra-general}
 \mathcal T-\mathcal S(H^f)
 \ge c_*\mathcal T-C_N(F^2+|K|^2).$$

There are fixed constants $c,C_*>0$ such that, for sufficiently large $N$ and the allowed truncations, the first two stages satisfy $$\begin{array}{ll}
 c/\tau\le\partial_\nu f\le C_*/\tau&\text{on black faces},\\[2pt]
 c/\tau\le-\partial_\nu f\le C_*/\tau&\text{on white faces}.
 \end{array}
 \label{n3:apriori:signed-slopes}$$ During the third stage one still has $|\partial_\nu f|\le C_*/\tau$.

For smooth $f,t$ satisfying <a href="#four:maximal:def:trace" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="four:maximal:def:trace">[four:maximal:def:trace]</a>, $$\label{four:maximal:def:scalar-identity}
 \frac12 e^{2t}R_{\widehat g}
 =\mu+J(w)+\mathcal T+w(F)-F\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}K-u^{-1}\mathop{\mathrm{div}}V,$$ where $$\begin{aligned}
\label{four:maximal:def:quadratic-form}
 \mathcal T={}&\tfrac12|T^0|^2+|M+(p+1)ay|^2-v|y|^2
       +3(p+1)(|y|^2+dx^2)\notag\\
 &-\tfrac13(F-\chi j)^2+\chi j^2-\chi Fj+F^2
       +2(p+1)\chi jax.
\end{aligned}$$ This expression extends smoothly across $df=0$.

At an interior minimum of $t$, $$\label{four:maximal:def:graph-gauss}
 \tfrac12e^{2t}R_{\widehat g}
 \le\tfrac12R_g-v\mathop{\mathrm{Ric}}_g(e_f,e_f)+\mathcal S(H^f),$$ where, for a symmetric tensor $A$, $$\label{four:maximal:def:gauss-form}
 \mathcal S(A)=\tfrac13(\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}_\perp A)^2
 -\tfrac12|A_\perp^0|^2
 +dA_{ee}\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}_\perp A-d|A_{e\perp}|^2.$$

The following estimates hold along every smooth trace solution: $$\label{four:maximal:def:derivative-bounds}
 |\nabla w|+|\nabla A_\chi|+|d\log u|_{A_\chi}
 \le\Pi_N(\sqrt{\mathcal T}+|K|).$$

For all sufficiently large $N$ and allowed $R$, there are fixed constants $0<c_5<C_6$ such that $$\label{four:maximal:bounds:collar-slopes}
 \frac{c_5}{\eta_N}\le\partial_\nu f\le\frac{C_6}{\eta_N}
 \quad\hbox{in stages 1 and 2},\qquad
 |\partial_\nu f|\le\frac{C_6}{\eta_N}
 \quad\hbox{in stage 3}.$$

For every sufficiently large fixed $N$ and all sufficiently large allowed $R$, every admissible smooth homotopy solution satisfies $$\label{four:maximal:bounds:bounded-range}
 |f|\le C_0/\eta_N,\qquad
 -\epsilon\le t\le Z\le C(N),\qquad |\mathop{\mathrm{grad}}f|\le C(N).$$

For the limiting solution at fixed $N$, the flux $$\mathfrak F=\lim_{r\to\infty}\int_{S_r}V\cdot\nu_g\,\mathrm dA_g$$ exists and satisfies $$\label{four:maximal:end:mass-flux}
 E_{\widehat g}=E_g-\frac{\mathfrak F}{3\omega_3},
 \qquad \omega_3=2\pi^2.$$

outputs.exact_statement:
Let $(\Omega,g,K)$ be an exterior as in Definition <a href="#n3:intro:exterior-class" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="n3:intro:exterior-class">1</a>. Assume Equations <a href="#n3:intro:constraints" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="n3:intro:constraints">[n3:intro:constraints]</a>–<a href="#n3:intro:decay" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="n3:intro:decay">[n3:intro:decay]</a>, integrability of $\mu$ and $|J|_g$, and the finite ADM limits <a href="#n3:intro:energy" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="n3:intro:energy">[n3:intro:energy]</a>–<a href="#n3:intro:momentum" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="n3:intro:momentum">[n3:intro:momentum]</a>. Orient $\partial\Omega$ by the normal into $\Omega$, and suppose $\theta_+(\partial\Omega)\le0$. If $E>|P|_\delta$, then $$\label{n3:intro:penrose}
          \sqrt{E^2-|P|_\delta^2}
               \ge \sqrt{\frac{a_g(\partial\Omega)}{16\pi}}.$$ No extension of the data across $\partial\Omega$ is required.

Let $E_c$, $c$ in an interval, be an increasing family of closed subsets of a fixed compact metric space. Outside a countable set of parameters, $$\label{n3:domains:hausdorff-right}
 E_{c_j}\longrightarrow E_c\quad\hbox{in Hausdorff distance whenever}
 \quad c_j\downarrow c.$$ Here continuity at $E_c=\varnothing$ means that $E_{c'}$ is empty for every sufficiently close $c'>c$.

Let $(N^d,g)$ be smooth, with $d\in\{3,4\}$ and a fixed smooth symmetric tensor $Q$. Let $\Sigma$ be a compact connected smooth two-sided hypersurface without boundary, forming a component of the frontier of a closed region $D$, with normal pointing out of $D$. Assume $\Theta_Q(\Sigma)=c$ and stability for outward variations. Assume also local *outward-replacement maximality*: in some collar of $\Sigma$ disjoint from the other frontier components and all designated barrier faces, no positive outward normal graph can replace $\Sigma$, leaving $D$ unchanged outside that collar, and have expansion at most $c$ everywhere on the new component. There is a smooth foliation $\Sigma_s$, $0\leq s<s_0$, of an outward collar, with $\Sigma_0=\Sigma$ and positive outward normal velocity, such that $$\label{n3:domains:collar-expansion}
 \Theta_Q(\Sigma_s)>c\quad(0<s<s_0),\qquad
 \Theta_Q(\Sigma_0)=c.$$ In particular its leaf parameter $s$ is a smooth defining function and $|\nabla s|$ is bounded above and below by positive constants.

Let $N$ be a smooth compact connected three-manifold with boundary $\Gamma_-\sqcup\Gamma_+$, where $\Gamma_+$ is nonempty and $\Gamma_-$ may be empty. Let $D$ be a nonempty closed subset which contains a relative collar of $\Gamma_-$ when that boundary is prescribed, and stays a positive distance from $\Gamma_+$. Suppose its interior frontier has exterior-support expansion at most $c$ for a smooth tensor $Q$.*


*For every $c'>c$ such that $\Theta_Q(\Gamma_+)>c'$ with its normal out of $N$, there is a smooth bounding domain $E$ containing $D$ in its relative interior, containing the required inner collar, and avoiding $\Gamma_+$, whose free boundary satisfies $$\label{n3:domains:smooth-enclosure-expansion}
 \Theta_Q(\partial E\setminus\Gamma_-)=c'.$$ Its boundary and the domain may be disconnected. In particular $D$ is contained in the closed total trapped region at any such larger threshold. No regularity or positive-thickness assumption on $D$ is required.

The thresholds and the domain above can be chosen with the following properties. The numbers $c_b,c_w<0$ are right-continuity points of their respective full maximal-region families. The boundary of $\Omega$ consists of the faces in Equation <a href="#n3:domains:face-expansions" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="n3:domains:face-expansions">[n3:domains:face-expansions]</a>, has disjoint smooth outward leaf collars as in Lemma <a href="#n3:domains:stable-collar" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="n3:domains:stable-collar">7</a>, and preserves the enclosing-area lower bound in Equation <a href="#n3:domains:inherited-area" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="n3:domains:inherited-area">[n3:domains:inherited-area]</a>.*


*There are a smooth compactly supported function $C$ on $\Omega$, constants $C_b\geq0$, $C_w\leq0$, and positive constants $k_B$ on its face collars such that $$\label{n3:domains:offset-collars}
 \begin{aligned}
  C_w\leq C\leq C_b,&\qquad
  C=C_b-k_Bs&&\text{in a black collar near }B_b,\\
  &\qquad C=C_w+k_Bs&&\text{in a white collar near }B_w.
 \end{aligned}$$ For an absent type, its corresponding constant is simply a bound. Set $$\label{n3:domains:assigned-heights}
 b_-=c_b-C_b<0,
 \qquad b_+=-c_w-C_w>0.$$ There is a smooth positive function $\rho$, equal to a positive constant times $r^{-4}$ on the far end and satisfying symbol derivative bounds there, such that, for every $h\in[b_-,b_+]$, $$\label{n3:domains:offset-smallness}
 |(h+C)\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}K|+|\nabla C|+\rho
      \leq\tfrac12\mathfrak m
 \quad\hbox{everywhere on }\Omega.$$ The thresholds, collars, offset, and $\rho$ are fixed before the deformation parameters are chosen. After fixing the thresholds and collars, the offset and its first derivatives can be made arbitrarily small.

Let $f,t$ be smooth and $F=\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}_{A_\chi}(K+H^f)$. At $\sigma>0$ decompose $S$ relative to $\mathbb Re\oplus e^\perp$: $$T=S|_{e^\perp\times e^\perp},\quad
 M=S(e,\cdot)|_{e^\perp},\quad q=S(e,e),\quad \mathop{\mathrm{tr}}T=F-\chi q.$$ Then $$\label{n3:deformation:scalar-identity}
 \begin{split}
 \tfrac12e^{4t}\mathop{\mathrm{Scal}}_{\hat g}
 ={}&8\pi(\mu+J(w))+\mathcal T
       +w(F)-F\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}K-u^{-1}\mathop{\mathrm{div}}V,
 \end{split}$$ where $$\label{n3:deformation:quadratic-term}
 \begin{split}
 \mathcal T={}&\tfrac12|T^{\operatorname{tf}}|^2
 +(M+pa\nabla_\perp t)\cdot(M+(p+2)a\nabla_\perp t)\\
 &+4(p+1)|\,\mathrm dt|_{A_d}^2
   +(\chi-\chi^2/4)q^2+2(p+1)\chi qa\,\partial_e t\\
 &-\chi Fq/2+3F^2/4.
 \end{split}$$ After substituting $F=\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}_{A_\chi}S$, this expression extends smoothly across $\sigma=0$.

The weighted coercivity theorem of (OpenAI 2026b, Proposition 4.2) [[NumericalCompanion](#ref-NumericalCompanion)], with Equation <a href="#n3:deformation:normalization-dictionary" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="n3:deformation:normalization-dictionary">[n3:deformation:normalization-dictionary]</a>, makes $\mathcal T$ nonnegative. For $0\le p\le N$, $$\label{n3:deformation:coercive-estimate}
 |\,\mathrm dt|_{A_d}^2+|T|^2+|M|^2+dq^2+F^2
 \le C(1+N)^A\mathcal T$$ with universal $C,A$. At $\,\mathrm dt=0$ there is the stronger comparison, uniform in $p,d$, $$\label{n3:deformation:floor-norm}
 c\bigl(|T^{\operatorname{tf}}|^2+|M|^2+\chi q^2+F^2\bigr)
 \le\mathcal T
 \le C\bigl(|T^{\operatorname{tf}}|^2+|M|^2+\chi q^2+F^2\bigr).$$

There are $r_0,C>0$, independent of $N,R$ and the homotopy parameter, such that for $R\ge2r_0$, $$\label{n3:deformation:end-height-estimate}
 |h|\le C(r^{-\gamma}-R^{-\gamma})\quad(r_0\le r\le R).$$ In particular, $$\label{n3:deformation:outer-gradient}
 |\nabla f|\le C R^{-1-\gamma}/\tau\quad\hbox{on }S_R.$$ For fixed $\epsilon,N$, a sufficiently large lower bound for $R$ gives $t>-\epsilon$ on $S_R$.

At an interior point where $\,\mathrm dt=0$ and $\mathop{\mathrm{Hess}}t\ge0$, $$\label{n3:deformation:floor-gauss}
 \tfrac12e^{4t}\mathop{\mathrm{Scal}}_{\hat g}
 \le \tfrac12\mathop{\mathrm{Scal}}_g-v\mathop{\mathrm{Ric}}_g(e,e)+\mathcal S(H^f),$$ where, for a symmetric tensor $A$, $$\label{n3:deformation:gauss-quadratic}
 \begin{split}
 \mathcal S(A)={}&\tfrac14(\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}_\perp A)^2
 -\tfrac12|A_{\perp\perp}^{\operatorname{tf}}|^2\\
 &+dA_{ee}\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}_\perp A-d|A_{e\perp}|^2.
 \end{split}$$ There are universal $c_*,\eta_*>0$ and polynomial constants $C_N$ such that, at $\,\mathrm dt=0$, $$\label{n3:deformation:floor-algebra-general}
 \mathcal T-\mathcal S(H^f)
 \ge c_*\mathcal T-C_N(F^2+|K|^2).$$ If $K=0$, the more useful uniform estimate is $$\label{n3:deformation:floor-algebra-end}
 \mathcal T-\mathcal S(H^f)\ge c_*\mathcal T-C_N vF^2.$$

There are fixed constants $c,C_*>0$ such that, for sufficiently large $N$ and the allowed truncations, the first two stages satisfy $$\begin{array}{ll}
 c/\tau\le\partial_\nu f\le C_*/\tau&\text{on black faces},\\[2pt]
 c/\tau\le-\partial_\nu f\le C_*/\tau&\text{on white faces}.
 \end{array}
 \label{n3:apriori:signed-slopes}$$ During the third stage one still has $|\partial_\nu f|\le C_*/\tau$. On every fixed compact region needed for the final scalar-curvature estimate, the first two stages satisfy $b_-\le h\le b_+$.

Let $U'$ be compactly contained in an interior or boundary coordinate patch $U$ in dimension three. In a boundary patch assume that $z$ is constant on the boundary face. Suppose $z$ is $C^3$, up to that face when present, $|\nabla z|\le L$, and, almost everywhere, $$\label{n3:existence:axial-equation}
 A_\chi:\mathop{\mathrm{Hess}}z=G,\qquad
 A_\chi=I-(1-\chi)e\otimes e,\qquad
 e=\frac{\nabla z}{|\nabla z|},\qquad
 0<\chi_0\le\chi\le1,$$ where $\|G\|_\infty\le Q$. At zero gradients any measurable unit axis for which the equation holds is allowed. Then there are $\alpha\in(0,1)$ and $C<\infty$, depending only on $\chi_0$, the patch geometry and the separation of $U'$ from the other patch boundaries, such that $$\label{n3:existence:gradient-estimate}
 \|\nabla z\|_{C^{0,\alpha}(U')}
 \le C(L+Q).$$ For all sufficiently small coordinate balls centered in $U'$, with intersection with the domain understood at a boundary, one also has $$\label{n3:existence:hessian-morrey}
 \int_{B_r\cap U}|\mathop{\mathrm{Hess}}z|^2\,\mathrm dV_g
 \le C(L+Q)^2r^{1+2\alpha}.$$ In particular, the constants are independent of continuity moduli of $\chi$ and $G$.

Fix $\epsilon>0$ and a sufficiently large $N\ge\max\{4,\lfloor2/\epsilon\rfloor+1\}$. The truncation solutions of Theorem <a href="#n3:existence:deformation" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="n3:existence:deformation">32</a> have a subsequence converging smoothly on compact subsets of $\overline\Omega$ to a solution of the physical system with $t\ge-\epsilon$. On the asymptotically flat end, for some $c_N>0$ and fixed-$N$ constants $C_j(N)$, $$|\nabla^j f|\le C_j(N)e^{-c_N r}
 \quad\text{for each fixed }j,\qquad
 Z,t=O_2(r^{-1}).$$ The differences $t-Z$, $\bar g-g$, and their coordinate derivatives of any fixed order decay exponentially. The vector field $V$ satisfies $$\label{n3:limit:V-asymptote}
                    V=4\nabla_g t+O(r^{-3})$$ and has a finite outward flux limit.

For the solution in Lemma <a href="#n3:limit:end" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="n3:limit:end">33</a>, $$\hat g=e^{4t}(g+l^2df^2)$$ is a smooth complete metric on $\overline\Omega$, with nonnegative scalar curvature and strictly negative mean curvature on $B$ for the normal pointing into $\Omega$. Its end is asymptotically flat with $$\hat g-\delta=O_2(r^{-1}),\qquad
 R_{\hat g}=O(r^{-3-\delta'}),\qquad
 \delta'=\min\{\delta,1\}>0,$$ where the reduced end has $R_g=O(r^{-3-\delta})$. If $$\mathfrak F_N=\lim_{r\to\infty}
                    \int_{S_r}V_\nu\,\mathrm dA_g,$$ then $$\label{n3:limit:adm}
                         E_{\hat g}=E-\frac{\mathfrak F_N}{8\pi}.$$

Let $(M,g,K)$ be a smooth connected orientable initial-data exterior, diffeomorphic to $\mathbb R^4$ minus an open ball, complete up to its connected compact boundary $S\cong S^3$, and with one asymptotically flat end. Here $K$ is a symmetric covariant two-tensor. Assume the vacuum constraint equations with zero cosmological constant and maximality: $$\label{four:maximal:intro:constraints}
 R_g+(\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}_gK)^2-|K|_g^2=0,\qquad
 \mathop{\mathrm{div}}_g\bigl(K-(\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}_gK)g\bigr)=0,\qquad \mathop{\mathrm{tr}}_gK=0.$$ Assume a smooth effective $T^2=U(1)\times U(1)$ action preserving $g,K$ which, in an asymptotically Euclidean chart $\mathbb R^4=\mathbb C^2$, is $$(e^{i\alpha},e^{i\beta})\cdot(z_1,z_2)
       =(e^{i\alpha}z_1,e^{i\beta}z_2)$$ at all sufficiently large radii. No cohomogeneity-one assumption is made. In that chart suppose $$\label{four:maximal:intro:decay}
 g_{ij}-\delta_{ij}=O_2(r^{-q}),\qquad
 K_{ij}=O_1(r^{-q-1}),\qquad q>1.$$ Suppose the following ADM limits exist and are finite: $$\begin{aligned}
 E&=\frac{1}{6\omega_3}\lim_{r\to\infty}
     \int_{|x|=r}(\partial_jg_{ij}-\partial_ig_{jj})
                  \nu_\delta^i\,\mathrm dA_\delta,\label{four:maximal:intro:energy}\\
 P_i&=\frac{1}{3\omega_3}\lim_{r\to\infty}
     \int_{|x|=r}\bigl(K_{ij}-(\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}_gK)g_{ij}\bigr)
                  \nu_\delta^j\,\mathrm dA_\delta.\label{four:maximal:intro:momentum}
\end{aligned}$$ Assume $P=0$ and $E>0$.*


*Orient $S$ by its unit normal $\nu$ toward the end and set $H_S=\mathop{\mathrm{div}}_S\nu$. Suppose $$\label{four:maximal:intro:mots}
 \Theta_K(S):=H_S+\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}_SK=0.$$ Suppose $S$ is outermost toward the end: there is no compact smooth embedded two-sided hypersurface in the interior enclosing $S$, oriented toward the end, with $\Theta_K\le0$ everywhere. Suppose also that $S$ is outer area-minimizing: every compact smooth embedded enclosing hypersurface has three-volume at least $A=|S|_g$. Disconnected and noninvariant enclosing competitors are included. Then $$\label{four:maximal:intro:main-inequality}
 E\ge\frac12\left(\frac{A}{2\pi^2}\right)^{2/3}.$$

For smooth $f,t$ satisfying <a href="#four:maximal:def:trace" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="four:maximal:def:trace">[four:maximal:def:trace]</a>, $$\label{four:maximal:def:scalar-identity}
 \frac12 e^{2t}R_{\widehat g}
 =\mu+J(w)+\mathcal T+w(F)-F\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}K-u^{-1}\mathop{\mathrm{div}}V,$$ where $$\begin{aligned}
\label{four:maximal:def:quadratic-form}
 \mathcal T={}&\tfrac12|T^0|^2+|M+(p+1)ay|^2-v|y|^2
       +3(p+1)(|y|^2+dx^2)\notag\\
 &-\tfrac13(F-\chi j)^2+\chi j^2-\chi Fj+F^2
       +2(p+1)\chi jax.
\end{aligned}$$ This expression extends smoothly across $df=0$.

The quadratic form in <a href="#four:maximal:def:quadratic-form" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="four:maximal:def:quadratic-form">[four:maximal:def:quadratic-form]</a> is nonnegative, and $$\label{four:maximal:def:coercivity}
 |T|^2+|M|^2+dj^2+F^2+|dt|_{A_d}^2\le\Pi_N\mathcal T.$$ At $dt=0$ it has the stronger equivalence $$\label{four:maximal:def:coercivity-stationary}
 \mathcal T\asymp |T^0|^2+|M|^2+\chi j^2+F^2,$$ with both constants absolute, independent of $N,d,\chi$.

Every smooth homotopy solution satisfies $|h|+|F|\le C_0$ for a fixed constant independent of $N,R$ and the homotopy parameter. There are fixed $r_0,C_2$ such that, for all sufficiently large $R$ depending on $N,\epsilon$, the first three stages satisfy $$\label{four:maximal:def:height-end}
 |h|\le C_2(r^{-\gamma}-R^{-\gamma})\quad(r_0\le r\le R),
 \qquad |\mathop{\mathrm{grad}}f|\big|_{S_R}\le C_3\eta_N^{-1}R^{-1-\gamma}.$$ Moreover $t>-\epsilon$ on $S_R$. None of these assertions assumes an interior lower bound for $t$.

At an interior minimum of $t$, $$\label{four:maximal:def:graph-gauss}
 \tfrac12e^{2t}R_{\widehat g}
 \le\tfrac12R_g-v\mathop{\mathrm{Ric}}_g(e_f,e_f)+\mathcal S(H^f),$$ where, for a symmetric tensor $A$, $$\label{four:maximal:def:gauss-form}
 \mathcal S(A)=\tfrac13(\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}_\perp A)^2
 -\tfrac12|A_\perp^0|^2
 +dA_{ee}\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}_\perp A-d|A_{e\perp}|^2.$$ At every point where $dt=0$, there is an absolute $c_2>0$ such that $$\label{four:maximal:def:floor-comparison}
 \mathcal T-\mathcal S(H^f)
 \ge c_2\mathcal T-\Pi_N\bigl(vF^2+|K|^2\bigr).$$ At $df=0$ the terms multiplied by $v$ vanish and any axis may be used.

The following estimates hold along every smooth trace solution: $$\label{four:maximal:def:derivative-bounds}
 |\nabla w|+|\nabla A_\chi|+|d\log u|_{A_\chi}
 \le\Pi_N(\sqrt{\mathcal T}+|K|).$$ Consequently, for each fixed $\eta_1>0$, $$\label{four:maximal:def:drift-divergence}
 \frac{|\mathop{\mathrm{div}}(uA_\chi K(w,\cdot))|}{u}
 \le\eta_1\mathcal T+\Pi_N\bigl(|K|^2+a|\nabla K|\bigr).$$ In each of the first three trace stages, $$\label{four:maximal:def:F-derivative}
 w(F)\ge\eta_N a\sigma-\eta_1\mathcal T
   -\Pi_N\bigl(\mathbf1_{\mathcal K}+|K|^2+a|\nabla K|\bigr),$$ where $\mathcal K$ is a fixed compact enlargement of the supports of $C,D_0,D_1$. For fixed $\eta_1$ the bounds remain polynomial in $N$.

Let $Q_0$ be a compact subset of $\overline\Omega$ disjoint from $\mathcal D_c$. There are $\eta_2>0$, $N_0$, and allowed radii $R_{\min}(N)\to\infty$ such that, in stages 1 and 2, $$\label{four:maximal:bounds:separation}
 h\ge c+\eta_2\quad\hbox{on }Q_0
 \qquad(N\ge N_0,\ R\ge R_{\min}(N)).$$ The constants are uniform over both stages.

For all sufficiently large $N$ and allowed $R$, there are fixed constants $0<c_5<C_6$ such that $$\label{four:maximal:bounds:collar-slopes}
 \frac{c_5}{\eta_N}\le\partial_\nu f\le\frac{C_6}{\eta_N}
 \quad\hbox{in stages 1 and 2},\qquad
 |\partial_\nu f|\le\frac{C_6}{\eta_N}
 \quad\hbox{in stage 3}.$$ Here $\nu$ points into $\Omega$. In stage 4, $f=0$.

Let $\mathcal C$ be a fixed union of collars. In stages 1 and 2, every nonnegative $C^1$ function $\varphi$ satisfies $$\begin{aligned}
 \int_B L_0\varphi
 &\le\Pi_N\int_{\mathcal C}
  u\bigl((1+\sqrt{\mathcal T})\varphi+|\,\mathrm d\varphi|_{A_\chi}\bigr),
       \label{four:maximal:bounds:trace-weighted}\\
 \int_B\varphi
 &\le\Pi_N\int_{\mathcal C}
  \sqrt D\bigl((1+\sqrt{\mathcal T})\varphi+|\,\mathrm d\varphi|_{A_\chi}\bigr).
       \label{four:maximal:bounds:trace-unweighted}
\end{aligned}$$ The integrands vanish outside the support of $\varphi$ and its derivative; thus both statements localize to any base patch. Moreover $$\label{four:maximal:bounds:boundary-flux}
 |(V_\lambda)_\nu|\le C_7ud=C_7L_0\sqrt d
                     \le C_8(\eta_N/\ell)L_0.$$ Consequently, $$\label{four:maximal:bounds:boundary-absorption}
 \int_B|(V_\lambda)_\nu|
 \le \Pi_N\frac{\eta_N}{\ell}
             \int_{\mathcal C}u(\rho+\delta_0\mathcal T).$$ All constants in these assertions have the stated polynomial control.

For every sufficiently large fixed $N$ and all sufficiently large allowed $R$, every admissible smooth homotopy solution satisfies $$\label{four:maximal:bounds:bounded-range}
 |f|\le C_0/\eta_N,\qquad
 -\epsilon\le t\le Z\le C(N),\qquad |\mathop{\mathrm{grad}}f|\le C(N).$$ The constants are independent of $R$, the solution, and the homotopy parameter. No uniform Hessian or coefficient continuity estimate is used in obtaining these bounds.

The exhaustion solutions of Proposition <a href="#four:maximal:prop:existence" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="four:maximal:prop:existence">58</a> admit a smooth local limit $(f,Z)$ on $\overline\Omega$ satisfying the desired system and $t\ge-\epsilon$. Every such limit has $$\label{four:maximal:end:decay}
 |\nabla^j f|\le C_j(N)e^{-c_j(N)r}\quad(j\ge0),
 \qquad Z,t=O_2(r^{-2}).$$ The zeroth-order estimates producing this normalization are uniform on the finite truncations. The resulting metric $\widehat g=e^{2t}(g+l^2df\otimes df)$ is smooth, complete with its boundary included, and asymptotically flat with $$\label{four:maximal:end:geometry}
 \widehat g_{ij}-\delta_{ij}=O_2(r^{-q}),\qquad
 0\le R_{\widehat g}=O(r^{-4-\delta}),\qquad \widehat H_B<0.$$ Here the normal defining $\widehat H_B$ points toward infinity.

For the limiting solution at fixed $N$, the flux $$\mathfrak F=\lim_{r\to\infty}\int_{S_r}V\cdot\nu_g\,\mathrm dA_g$$ exists and satisfies $$\label{four:maximal:end:mass-flux}
 E_{\widehat g}=E_g-\frac{\mathfrak F}{3\omega_3},
 \qquad \omega_3=2\pi^2.$$ There is a polynomial bound $\Pi_N$, independent of the exhaustion radius and of the fixed-$N$ regularity constants, such that $$\label{four:maximal:end:flux-deficit}
 \mathfrak F\ge-\Pi_N\left(\ell+\frac{\ell^2}{\eta_N}\right).$$ Consequently, for fixed strict data and $\epsilon>0$, $E_{\widehat g}\le E_g+o(1)$ as $N\to\infty$.
